  <!-- ── III. PROCLAIM LIBERTY ─────────────────────────────────── -->
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-eyebrow" data-lang-en>American Echo</div>
    <div class="section-eyebrow" data-lang-es>Eco Estadounidense</div>
    <div class="section-title">Proclaim Liberty</div>

    <div class="torah-section">
      <div class="torah-hebrew">וְקָרָאתֶם דְּרוֹר בָּאָרֶץ לְכָל יוֹשְׁבֶיהָ</div>
      <div class="torah-transliteration"><em>V'karatam d'ror ba'aretz l'khol yosveha</em></div>
      <div class="torah-english" data-lang-en>"Proclaim liberty throughout all the land unto all the inhabitants thereof"</div>
      <div class="torah-english" data-lang-es>"Proclama libertad por toda la tierra a todos sus habitantes"</div>
      <div class="torah-ref">Leviticus 25:10 · Inscribed on the Liberty Bell, Philadelphia</div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-desc" data-lang-en>
      These words from the Torah were inscribed upon the Liberty Bell — the voice of freedom itself. As the shofar awoke ancient Israel, so did the Liberty Bell awaken America. Throughout our nation's history, the bell has been struck to announce freedom, summon citizens to civic action, and mark moments of liberation.
    </div>
    <div class="section-desc" data-lang-es>
      Estas palabras de la Torá fueron inscritas en la Campana de la Libertad — la voz de la libertad misma. Así como el shofar despertó a Israel antiguo, la Campana de la Libertad despertó a América.
    </div>

    <div class="section-desc" style="font-weight:600;margin-bottom:8px;">
      <span data-lang-en>June 6, 1944 · D-Day</span>
      <span data-lang-es>6 de junio de 1944 · Día D</span>
    </div>

    <div class="section-desc" data-lang-en>
      On the morning of the Allied invasion of Normandy, Philadelphia Mayor Bernard Samuel struck the Liberty Bell <strong>seven times</strong> — once for each letter in the word "LIBERTY" — during a nationwide radio broadcast. Each strike symbolized freedom's advance. That day, Europe began its return to liberty.
    </div>
    <div class="section-desc" data-lang-es>
      En la mañana de la invasión aliada de Normandía, el alcalde de Filadelfia Bernard Samuel tocó la Campana de la Libertad <strong>siete veces</strong> — una por cada letra de la palabra "LIBERTAD."
    </div>

    <div class="section-desc" style="margin-top:20px;padding-top:20px;border-top:1px solid var(--rule);">
      <strong data-lang-en>Historical Purposes for Striking the Bell</strong>
      <strong data-lang-es>Propósitos Históricos del Toque de Campana</strong>
      <br><br>
      <strong>Civic Announcements:</strong> The bell summoned lawmakers to the Pennsylvania Assembly and called residents together for public meetings and town forums. It was the voice of collective decision-making.
      <br><br>
      <strong>Public Warnings:</strong> The bell signaled emergencies — local fires, approaching danger, invasion threats during the Revolutionary War era. It was the alarm that unified a community in crisis.
      <br><br>
      <strong>Commemorations:</strong> The bell tolled for the deaths of great leaders (George Washington, Benjamin Franklin) and marked the anniversaries of liberation. It was the voice of collective grief and remembrance.
    </div>

