  <!-- ── XVIII. THE THIRTEEN COLONIES ──────────────────────────── -->
  <div class="section" style="border-radius:0 0 8px 8px;">
    <div class="section-eyebrow">Historical Context</div>
    <div class="section-title">The Thirteen Colonies</div>
    <div class="section-desc">
      How Divided We Were: Religious Requirements, Ethnic Restrictions, and the Improbable Union That Became the United States
    </div>
    <div class="section-desc" style="color:var(--ink-mut);font-size:13px;">
      Thirteen colonies with different churches, charters, and ethnic compositions agreed — first on independence, then on a constitution. That consensus was extraordinary. It required compromise, sacrifice, and the best political thinking of an era to forge unity from genuine, material difference.
    </div>

    <button class="expand-toggle" data-target="colonies-detail" onclick="toggleColonies(this)">
      <span class="expand-arrow">▼</span>
      <span class="expand-label">View Full Comparison</span>
    </button>

    <div class="colonies-content" id="colonies-detail">

      <div class="colonies-intro">
        <p>In 1776, thirteen disparate colonies on the Atlantic seaboard declared themselves a unified nation. But they were anything but unified. Each had been established with different charters, different religious mandates, and different visions of who belonged and who was excluded. Some colonies mandated allegiance to a state church. Others prohibited Catholics, Jews, or non-believers from holding office. Some required land ownership; others restricted it. Some welcomed immigrants; others built walls.</p>
        <p>The act of creating "The United States" required these thirteen profoundly different societies to set aside their constitutions, their religious establishments, and their parochial interests. They had to imagine a nation where no single faith dominated, where citizenship transcended ethnic origin, and where the rights of conscience superseded the edicts of governors and bishops.</p>
        <p>This chart documents that diversity. It shows not only what divided us, but what we were brave enough to overcome.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="colonies-table-wrap">
        <table class="colonies-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Colony</th>
              <th>Settled / Founded</th>
              <th>Charter / Founding Document</th>
              <th>Religious Requirements &amp; Restrictions</th>
              <th>Ethnic / Land Composition</th>
              <th>Governance Structure</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td class="col-cname">Virginia</td>
              <td>1607 · Jamestown</td>
              <td style="font-family:'JetBrains Mono',monospace;font-size:11px;">Virginia Company Charter (1606)<br>Royal Charter (1609)</td>
              <td>
                <div class="crestrict"><span class="crestrict-lbl">Mandatory</span>Church of England (Anglican) established as state church. All inhabitants required to attend services and pay tithes.</div>
                <div class="crestrict"><span class="crestrict-lbl">Prohibited</span>Catholics could not hold public office (1642).</div>
              </td>
              <td>English gentry, merchants, indentured servants. Indigenous dispossession (Powhatan Confederacy). Enslaved Africans imported after 1619.</td>
              <td>House of Burgesses (1619) — first representative assembly in America. Governor appointed by Crown; House elected by property-holding white males.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="col-cname">Massachusetts</td>
              <td>1620 · Plymouth; 1630 · Boston</td>
              <td style="font-family:'JetBrains Mono',monospace;font-size:11px;">Mayflower Compact (1620)<br>Massachusetts Bay Company Charter (1629)</td>
              <td>
                <div class="crestrict"><span class="crestrict-lbl">Mandatory</span>Congregational (Puritan) church established. Only "visible saints" could vote. Compulsory attendance; tithes required.</div>
                <div class="crestrict"><span class="crestrict-lbl">Prohibited</span>Catholics, Quakers, Anglicans, and "heretics" banished. Quakers executed (1659–1661).</div>
              </td>
              <td>Puritan English. Indigenous dispossession and conflict. Slavery later permitted (1630s–1640s).</td>
              <td>Congregational self-governance. Governor and magistrates appointed by General Court of male church members. Town meetings for local decisions.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="col-cname">New Hampshire</td>
              <td>1623 · Portsmouth</td>
              <td style="font-family:'JetBrains Mono',monospace;font-size:11px;">Royal Commission (1679)<br>Separate Crown Colony (1679)</td>
              <td>
                <div class="crestrict"><span class="crestrict-lbl">Mandatory</span>Congregational church established. Tithes and compulsory attendance.</div>
                <div class="ctolerance"><span class="ctolerance-lbl">Later Tolerance</span>More permissive than Massachusetts; Anglicans tolerated by 1700s.</div>
              </td>
              <td>English settlers; some Scots. Indigenous conflict (King Philip's War, 1675–1678). Slavery present but limited.</td>
              <td>Royal governor. House of Representatives (elected by property holders). Council appointed.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="col-cname">Rhode Island</td>
              <td>1636 · Providence (Roger Williams);<br>1638 · Newport</td>
              <td style="font-family:'JetBrains Mono',monospace;font-size:11px;">Rhode Island and Providence Plantations Charter (1663)</td>
              <td>
                <div class="ctolerance"><span class="ctolerance-lbl">Religious Freedom</span>No established church. No religious test for voting or office. Quakers, Baptists, Jews, and religious dissenters welcome. Conscience protected by charter.</div>
              </td>
              <td>English religious exiles; Quakers; Jews (first Jewish congregation in America, Newport, 1763). Slavery present.</td>
              <td>Elected governor and General Assembly. Most democratic of the thirteen colonies. Religious liberty encoded in 1663 charter.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="col-cname">Connecticut</td>
              <td>1636 · Hartford;<br>1638 · New Haven</td>
              <td style="font-family:'JetBrains Mono',monospace;font-size:11px;">Fundamental Orders (1638)<br>Royal Charter (1662)</td>
              <td>
                <div class="crestrict"><span class="crestrict-lbl">Mandatory</span>Congregational church established. Tithes and attendance compulsory. Church membership required for full citizenship.</div>
                <div class="crestrict"><span class="crestrict-lbl">Prohibited</span>Non-Congregationalists restricted from voting and office initially.</div>
              </td>
              <td>English Puritan settlers. Indigenous dispossession (Pequot War, 1637). Slavery introduced but not dominant.</td>
              <td>Elected governor by General Assembly. Theocratic oligarchy: magistrates and ministers jointly governed.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="col-cname">New York</td>
              <td>1624 · New Amsterdam (Dutch);<br>1664 · English conquest</td>
              <td style="font-family:'JetBrains Mono',monospace;font-size:11px;">Dutch West India Company Charter (1624)<br>English Royal Charter (1664, 1685)</td>
              <td>
                <div class="ctolerance"><span class="ctolerance-lbl">Dutch Era (to 1664)</span>Dutch Reformed privileged but some tolerance for Catholics, Jews, Quakers.</div>
                <div class="crestrict"><span class="crestrict-lbl">English Era (1664+)</span>Anglican church established. Non-conformists taxed for support but permitted to worship.</div>
              </td>
              <td>Dutch merchants; English, French, German, African (enslaved and free), Jewish, and Indigenous peoples. Most diverse major city in America by 1700.</td>
              <td>Dutch: appointed director-general and council. English: royal governor, appointed council, elected Assembly (from 1683).</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="col-cname">New Jersey</td>
              <td>1664 · Berkeley and Carteret grant</td>
              <td style="font-family:'JetBrains Mono',monospace;font-size:11px;">Berkeley &amp; Carteret Proprietorship (1664)<br>Royal Charter (1702)</td>
              <td>
                <div class="ctolerance"><span class="ctolerance-lbl">Proprietor Tolerance</span>No established church. Various Protestant denominations and Quakers welcomed. Religious test only for office (belief in God).</div>
              </td>
              <td>English, Dutch, Scots-Irish settlers. Quakers significant. Slavery present but agriculture-based.</td>
              <td>Proprietary colony. Royal governor after 1702. House of Representatives (elected). Council appointed.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="col-cname">Pennsylvania</td>
              <td>1681 · Philadelphia (William Penn)</td>
              <td style="font-family:'JetBrains Mono',monospace;font-size:11px;">Pennsylvania Charter of Privileges (1681, 1701)</td>
              <td>
                <div class="ctolerance"><span class="ctolerance-lbl">Religious Freedom (Official Policy)</span>No established church. All Protestants and Catholics tolerated. Jews tolerated. Religious belief (not specific sect) required for office. Most religiously pluralistic colony.</div>
              </td>
              <td>English Quakers, Welsh, German, Scots-Irish, French, Jewish settlers. Slavery permitted but Quaker conscience limited its expansion.</td>
              <td>Proprietary (Penn family). Elected Assembly (very democratic). Religious minorities participated in governance.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="col-cname">Delaware</td>
              <td>1638 · Fort Christina (Swedish);<br>1664 Dutch; 1682 English (Penn)</td>
              <td style="font-family:'JetBrains Mono',monospace;font-size:11px;">Swedish Crown Charter (1638)<br>Pennsylvania Charter (1681, 1701)</td>
              <td>
                <div class="ctolerance"><span class="ctolerance-lbl">Multi-Confessional</span>Swedish Lutheran → Dutch Reformed → English tolerance. No established church by 1701. Religious minorities welcome.</div>
              </td>
              <td>Swedish (original settlers), Dutch, English, Finns, Germans, Scots-Irish. Slavery introduced later.</td>
              <td>Initially Swedish/Dutch. Under Penn: separate legislature from 1701. Elected Assembly; appointed governor-general.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="col-cname">Maryland</td>
              <td>1634 · St. Mary's City (Lord Baltimore)</td>
              <td style="font-family:'JetBrains Mono',monospace;font-size:11px;">Maryland Charter (1632, proprietary)</td>
              <td>
                <div class="ctolerance"><span class="ctolerance-lbl">Catholic Proprietor, But…</span>Lord Baltimore (Catholic) initially tolerated Catholics and Protestants. 1649 Toleration Act protected Christian worship.</div>
                <div class="crestrict"><span class="crestrict-lbl">Later Restriction (1692+)</span>After Glorious Revolution, Anglican church established. Catholics increasingly restricted; anti-Catholic laws by 1704.</div>
              </td>
              <td>English Catholics (initial), then English Protestants. Scots-Irish later. Large enslaved African population (plantation economy).</td>
              <td>Proprietary (Lord Baltimore). Appointed governor. Elected House of Delegates. 1649 Act of Toleration granted broad Christian freedom.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="col-cname">North Carolina</td>
              <td>1663 · Albemarle Region (proprietary)</td>
              <td style="font-family:'JetBrains Mono',monospace;font-size:11px;">Carolina Charter (1663, 1665)</td>
              <td>
                <div class="crestrict"><span class="crestrict-lbl">Establishment Delayed</span>No established church initially. Anglican church eventually established (1701) with tax support. Some toleration for dissenters.</div>
              </td>
              <td>English settlers, Scots-Irish, German farmers. Large enslaved African population. Indigenous dispossession (Tuscarora War, 1711–1713).</td>
              <td>Proprietary colony. Appointed governor. Elected House of Burgesses. More frontier-like settlement than Virginia.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="col-cname">South Carolina</td>
              <td>1670 · Charleston</td>
              <td style="font-family:'JetBrains Mono',monospace;font-size:11px;">Carolina Charter (1663, 1665)</td>
              <td>
                <div class="crestrict"><span class="crestrict-lbl">Establishment</span>Anglican church established (1706). Strict Church establishment with tax support.</div>
                <div class="crestrict"><span class="crestrict-lbl">Prohibited</span>Catholics and non-Protestants restricted from office and in some periods from residence.</div>
              </td>
              <td>English planters and merchants. Massive enslaved African population — majority by 1708, a unique demographic inversion. Huguenots initially tolerated. Indigenous dispossession.</td>
