                <div class="ctolerance"><span class="ctolerance-lbl">Inclusive Intent (Initially)</span>Oglethorpe intended religious pluralism. Anglicans, Quakers, Jews, and others welcomed. No establishment initially; Anglican church later privileged (1750s).</div>
              </td>
              <td>English settlers, Scottish Highlanders, German Salzburgers (Lutherans), Sephardic and Ashkenazi Jews (1733+). Slavery prohibited initially; restriction lifted 1751.</td>
              <td>Proprietary (Trustees). No elected assembly initially. Appointed president and council. Assembly created 1751. Social experiment: military buffer against Spanish Florida.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="colonies-notes">
        <div class="colonies-notes-title">Key Takeaways: How We Overcame Our Differences</div>
        <div class="cnote">
          <span class="cnote-lbl">Religious Diversity</span>
          <div class="cnote-text">By 1776, the thirteen colonies spanned the full spectrum: mandatory established churches (Virginia, Massachusetts, Connecticut, North Carolina, South Carolina), proprietor tolerance (New York, New Jersey, Pennsylvania, Maryland), and religious freedom (Rhode Island). The Declaration and Constitution had to navigate between theocracies and sanctuaries. The First Amendment was the price of unity.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="cnote">
          <span class="cnote-lbl">Governance Structures</span>
          <div class="cnote-text">Each colony had evolved radically different systems: royal colonies (Virginia, New York, North Carolina), proprietary colonies (Pennsylvania, Maryland, Georgia), chartered corporations (Massachusetts, Connecticut, Rhode Island). The federal system of 1787 was a compromise that had never been attempted before at this scale.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="cnote">
          <span class="cnote-lbl">Ethnic &amp; Demographic Reality</span>
          <div class="cnote-text">The colonies were NOT homogeneous. New York was a Dutch-English-African-Jewish polyglot. Pennsylvania was a refuge for religious minorities. South Carolina was a Black-majority slave society. The myth of uniform "English" colonization obscures the demographic complexity that Union had to accommodate.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="cnote">
          <span class="cnote-lbl">The Enslaved African Reality</span>
          <div class="cnote-text">Slavery was woven into the fabric differently across colonies. Virginia institutionalized it (1640s+). New England used it less but profited from the slave trade. South Carolina became majority-Black and plantation-driven. This profound injustice was the <em>unresolved</em> difference that led to civil war 84 years after the Constitution.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="cnote">
          <span class="cnote-lbl">The Miracle of Consensus</span>
          <div class="cnote-text">That thirteen colonies with different churches, governors, charters, and ethnic compositions agreed on independence — and then on a constitution — was extraordinary. It required compromise on slavery, on representation, on religious tests (gradually abandoned). It required the best political thinking of an era to forge unity from genuine, material difference.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="cnote">
          <span class="cnote-lbl">Legacy in the Constitutional Moment</span>
          <div class="cnote-text">The Constitution's constraints on religious establishment, its separation of powers, and its protections for conscience were not ideological fantasies — they were hard-won pragmatic solutions to the real, documented differences in this table. Each restriction in the Constitution traces back to a conflict between colonies. Each protection traces back to a minority that had been persecuted or excluded.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div><!-- /colonies-content -->
  </div>

</div><!-- /container -->

